[
    {
        "content": "<p>Hi! Consider the following example:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">lemma</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">geometricMeasure_nonneg</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">positivity</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">pow_nonneg</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">linarith</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>If I remove <code>[pow_nonneg (a := 1 - p) (by linarith) n]</code> then <code>positivity</code> fails. I would like to know if it is possible to extend the <code>positivity</code> tactic to avoid that. I am not sure it is because of the <code>linarith</code> needed. Also I don't know how an extension of <code>positivity</code> is supposed to look like, it seems that it requires some metaprogramming, which I am not familiar with.</p>",
        "id": 578126725,
        "sender_full_name": "Etienne Marion",
        "timestamp": 1773054136
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>This works:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">lemma</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">geometricMeasure_nonneg</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℕ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">n</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">positivity</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">sub_nonneg_of_le</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h2</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>There has been discussion before about whether <code>positivity</code> should be able to use <code>sub_nonneg_of_le</code>. I think it should.</p>",
        "id": 578129843,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1773055034
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks for your answer! Do you know what the arguments against this are?</p>",
        "id": 578152866,
        "sender_full_name": "Etienne Marion",
        "timestamp": 1773061996
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>scope creep primarily, and performance as a downstream consequence of that.</p>",
        "id": 578167569,
        "sender_full_name": "Jireh Loreaux",
        "timestamp": 1773065516
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Here's a reason why it should be supported. We want <code>positivity</code> to be a flexible tactict. Hence, if <code>positivity</code> succeeds, then <code>simp at *; positivity</code> should also succeed. But this is violated</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">at</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- Remove this to make `positivity` work</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">positivity</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 578177220,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1773067772
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>At the moment, <code>positivity</code> is considered not flexible. I think changing this could be nice.</p>",
        "id": 578178702,
        "sender_full_name": "Michael Rothgang",
        "timestamp": 1773068151
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"479299\">@Jovan Gerbscheid</span> Personally, I wouldn't complain if you implemented <a href=\"#narrow/channel/113488-general/topic/.22Missing.20Tactics.22.20list/near/516741394\">https://leanprover.zulipchat.com/#narrow/channel/113488-general/topic/.22Missing.20Tactics.22.20list/near/516741394</a> <span aria-label=\"smiley\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f603\" role=\"img\" title=\"smiley\">:smiley:</span></p>",
        "id": 578179210,
        "sender_full_name": "Jireh Loreaux",
        "timestamp": 1773068270
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The issue with that is that we would have a new tactic doing mostly the same existing tactics. I think it may be better to expand the scope of existing tactics.</p>",
        "id": 578180532,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1773068541
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Though I guess what would make sense is something like <code>positivity (disch := linarith)</code>, or <code>positivity (disch := gcongr)</code></p>",
        "id": 578180939,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1773068616
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>yes, I agee it shouldn't really be a new tactic. I called it one there, but I would personally prefer the scope creep.</p>",
        "id": 578181481,
        "sender_full_name": "Jireh Loreaux",
        "timestamp": 1773068741
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Of course, I'm only one maintainer, so you should get buy-in from others before embarking on the work of actually doing it, if you are so inclined</p>",
        "id": 578181646,
        "sender_full_name": "Jireh Loreaux",
        "timestamp": 1773068780
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I've made <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/36401\">#36401</a> which adds a <code>positivity</code> extension for subtraction which looks for assumptions in the local context that prove the positivity.</p>",
        "id": 578272761,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1773098856
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I'm a longtime skeptic of letting <code>positivity</code> handle subtraction ... In particular, <code>positivity</code> is used ubiquitously as a discharger -- there are far more \"invisible\" uses of <code>positivity</code> than \"visible\" uses -- and it is important that the tactic stay super-fast and super-predictable.</p>",
        "id": 578277240,
        "sender_full_name": "Heather Macbeth",
        "timestamp": 1773101528
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Maybe there could be a flag, like <code>positivity -subtraction</code> that dischargers are expected to use?</p>",
        "id": 578330447,
        "sender_full_name": "Damiano Testa",
        "timestamp": 1773130925
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>No, but I want this to be used by dischargers. It's very useful!</p>",
        "id": 578354804,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1773137975
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(deleted)</p>",
        "id": 578361646,
        "sender_full_name": "Bhavik Mehta",
        "timestamp": 1773139828
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Your example does not fail.</p>",
        "id": 578362699,
        "sender_full_name": "Etienne Marion",
        "timestamp": 1773140125
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Oops, I had the description exactly reversed, fixed, thanks!</p>",
        "id": 578375557,
        "sender_full_name": "Bhavik Mehta",
        "timestamp": 1773144030
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I still don't get it? <code>simp</code> does nothing, and <code>simp [*]</code> closes the goal.</p>",
        "id": 578376013,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1773144153
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>My example was bad, sorry for the noise.</p>",
        "id": 578376490,
        "sender_full_name": "Bhavik Mehta",
        "timestamp": 1773144281
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The subtraction extension is a new \"base case\" for positivity (invoking hypotheses, rather than invoking <code>positivity</code> itself on subterms), which is the most fragile kind of change to <code>positivity</code>.</p>\n<p>In general, we may sometimes choose for a tactic to do <em>less</em>, if it means that the set of goals solved becomes more predictable. Here's an example of unpredictability that would result from the subtraction extension: if we have a hypothesis <code>h : a &lt; b</code> and a proof</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">stuff₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">stuff₂</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">algebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">involving</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">denominator</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">stuff₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">stuff₁</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">field</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>and <code>simp</code> changes to do something nontrivial to <code>stuff₂</code> (where previously it didn't), then the proof will break because  <code>field</code> will no longer be able to clear the denominator <code>stuff₂ - stuff₁</code> (because the hidden <code>positivity</code> will no longer discharge its nonzeroness).</p>",
        "id": 578412677,
        "sender_full_name": "Heather Macbeth",
        "timestamp": 1773153156
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I do not understand your point though, because on current mathlib, I would go about your example as follows:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">stuff₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">stuff₂</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">algebra</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">involving</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">denominator</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">stuff₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">stuff₁</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">have</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">sub_pos_of_lt</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">field</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>This is more difficult to write, because I have to remember this lemma name, and it is at least as fragile.</p>",
        "id": 578416576,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1773153934
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>It is easier to diagnose/fix though, because the likely point of failure of the <code>simp</code> change is visible in the code.</p>",
        "id": 578418475,
        "sender_full_name": "Heather Macbeth",
        "timestamp": 1773154407
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>(Also I'd use automation rather than a lemma!)</p>",
        "id": 578418521,
        "sender_full_name": "Heather Macbeth",
        "timestamp": 1773154416
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>But the subtraction here doesn't really matter to your point about fragility. There is the same fragility for every positivity goal that is discharged with a hypothesis.</p>",
        "id": 578419869,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1773154745
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yes, this is what I meant when I said that \"base cases\" for <code>positivity</code> are the most fragile kind of <code>positivity</code> extension, and that we should carefully limit how many we provide.</p>",
        "id": 578420966,
        "sender_full_name": "Heather Macbeth",
        "timestamp": 1773154998
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I would argue that usual discharging with hypotheses is many times more common than uses of this subtraction extension, so it shouldn't affect the fragility much. Are there any other such \"base cases\" that you are talking about?</p>",
        "id": 578423836,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1773155666
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"479299\">Jovan Gerbscheid</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Can.20I.20make.20positivity.20work.20for.20this.3F/near/578423836\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Are there any other such \"base cases\" that you are talking about?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>I can think of two, <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Mathlib.Meta.Positivity.compareHyp#doc\">docs#Mathlib.Meta.Positivity.compareHyp</a> and <a href=\"https://leanprover-community.github.io/mathlib4_docs/find/?pattern=Mathlib.Meta.Positivity.evalFinsetCard#doc\">docs#Mathlib.Meta.Positivity.evalFinsetCard</a></p>",
        "id": 578429183,
        "sender_full_name": "Heather Macbeth",
        "timestamp": 1773156927
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I'd distinguish fragility from predictability -- fragility is one component of (un)predictability. Adding a new, less commonly-used base case to <code>positivity</code> decreases the predictability of the tactic.</p>",
        "id": 578430529,
        "sender_full_name": "Heather Macbeth",
        "timestamp": 1773157228
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I personally wouldn't classify the new extension an unpredictable, because I think most mathematicians will tell you the same answer if you ask them \"under what condition is <code>a - b</code> positive?\".</p>",
        "id": 578468589,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1773167049
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I guess the point is that tactics are unpredictable if it is non-confluent and adding a new base case has a high chance of increasing non-confluent-ness.</p>",
        "id": 578472487,
        "sender_full_name": "Andrew Yang",
        "timestamp": 1773168310
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"479299\">Jovan Gerbscheid</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/287929-mathlib4/topic/Can.20I.20make.20positivity.20work.20for.20this.3F/near/578177220\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>Here's a reason why it should be supported. We want <code>positivity</code> to be a flexible tactict. Hence, if <code>positivity</code> succeeds, then <code>simp at *; positivity</code> should also succeed. But this is violated</p>\n<p><div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">h₂</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">simp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">at</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">h₁</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- Remove this to make `positivity` work</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">positivity</span>\n</code></pre></div><br>\n</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Doesn't this show that we're decreasing non-confluent-ness?</p>",
        "id": 578472812,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1773168425
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>My claim is still that we should have some way of discharging the following goal automatically, whether by <code>positivity</code>, <code>gcongr</code>, or some hybrid. The <code>positivity</code> extension Jovan wrote above helps with that <em>slightly</em>, but not enough.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ht</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hx</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ht_pos</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"bp\">⁻¹</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"bp\">⁻¹</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"k\">have</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">x</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">linarith</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">refine</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">mul_nonneg</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">positivity</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">?_</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">rw</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">sub_nonneg</span><span class=\"o\">]</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">gcongr</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 578508373,
        "sender_full_name": "Jireh Loreaux",
        "timestamp": 1773183613
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I think the main thing we would need to do to get that to work is to allow both <code>positivity</code> and <code>gcongr</code> to have custom dischargers, in the sense of a <code>(disch := ...)</code> argument, which I think both don't allow. Once we have that, we could simply write</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">macro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"cool_tactic\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">tactic</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">tactic</span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">first</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">gcongr</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">disch</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cool_tactic</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">positivity</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">disch</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cool_tactic</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">linarith</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 578509003,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1773183937
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Actually, for <code>gcongr</code>, we don't need the <code>(disch := ...)</code> option, because it creates new goals, so we would rather want something like</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">macro</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"s2\">\"cool_tactic\"</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">tactic</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">=&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">`</span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">tactic</span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">first</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">gcongr</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;;&gt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cool_tactic</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">positivity</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">disch</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">cool_tactic</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">linarith</span><span class=\"o\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 578509220,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1773184086
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The problem with giving <code>positivity</code> a discharger is that this is not really compatible with how <code>positivity</code> works. <code>positivity</code> works, in a sense, bottom-up, determining the positiveness of each subexpression (positive, nonnegative or nonzero), so you'd need to run the discharger on all three possible goals.</p>\n<p>I could imagine this being changed, but that would be a very fundamental change to how <code>positivity</code> works.</p>",
        "id": 578509780,
        "sender_full_name": "Jovan Gerbscheid",
        "timestamp": 1773184485
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"260507\">@Heather Macbeth</span> I'd argue that in certain circumstances, <code>positivity</code>'s limited scope actually <em>increases</em> its unpredictability. Consider the following examples which a new user (or even an experienced one) might encounter in order and be very confused. I know why the last one fails, but I think you can agree that this is an obscure problem. </p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">import</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Mathlib</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- `positivity` happily uses local hypothesis</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hp</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ht</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">p</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">t</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">positivity</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- works</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- `positivity` can handle summation too, nice!</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Finset</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∑</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"bp\">|</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">positivity</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- works</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">-- and so surely it can do both?</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">Type</span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">Finset</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ι</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">→</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hf</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∀</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∑</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">∈</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">s</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">i</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">positivity</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- fails</span>\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 578652141,
        "sender_full_name": "Jireh Loreaux",
        "timestamp": 1773238536
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>So I'm coming here from reviewing <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/36401\">#36401</a>. <span class=\"user-mention\" data-user-id=\"260507\">@Heather Macbeth</span>,  I'm now unsure if your point in this conversation is rather </p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"we should carefully consider whether there will be problems before adding support for subtraction\"</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>or</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>\"I'm convinced it is a bad idea, you shouldn't do that\".</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Could you maybe repeat your thoughts on the matter, please? What would be your suggestion on how we should proceed with <a href=\"https://github.com/leanprover-community/mathlib4/pull/36401\">#36401</a>?</p>\n<p>One observation I made now is that the test file of the tactic already contains</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Lean4\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℤ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">ha</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hb</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">positivity</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">sub_pos_of_lt</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ha</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">sub_pos_of_lt</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hb</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- the PR reduces this to `positivity`</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℚ</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">positivity</span>\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"mi\">4</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">2</span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"mi\">3</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">α</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≠</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">positivity</span>\n\n<span class=\"kn\">example</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">{</span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">ℝ</span><span class=\"o\">}</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hab</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">*</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hb</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">≤</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">hcd</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">&lt;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">^</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">/</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">(</span><span class=\"n\">d</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"bp\">-</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c</span><span class=\"o\">)</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">:=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">by</span>\n<span class=\"w\">  </span><span class=\"n\">positivity</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">[</span><span class=\"n\">sub_pos_of_lt</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hcd</span><span class=\"o\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">pos_of_mul_pos_left</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hab</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">hb</span><span class=\"o\">]</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"c1\">-- PR simplifies this too</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>which might be an indication that it already has been used for subtraction before?</p>",
        "id": 582237698,
        "sender_full_name": "Jon Eugster",
        "timestamp": 1774635315
    }
]